  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1208 PM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nThe freeze watch was upgraded to a warning for tonight for all\nof central and western Kansas. South winds will return to far\nwestern Kansas toward morning; but light winds are expected for\nsouth central Kansas through sunrise. Therefore, temperatures \nmay get as cold in the river bottoms near Dodge City, Cimarron \nand Jetmore as the traditionally colder spots in west central \nKansas. The long term average first freeze date is 5 to 7 days \nearlier in the low lying areas than the hilltops. Thus, expect \ntemperatures to be several degrees colder in these areas. \nTemperatures may stay several degrees warmer on the hill at the \nDodge City airport where the official low temperature for Dodge\nCity is taken.\n\nBroad southwesterly flow at mid and upper levels will return by\nthis weekend and into next week, allowing for surface troughing\nin the lee of the Rockies, south winds and warmer weather.\nExpect highs in the 70s by Monday and lower 80s by Wednesday.\n\nA large upper level trough will form across the southwestern \nUnited States late next week; and this system will slowly \nprogress eastward. However, this far west in the plains we\ngenerally need a slow moving upper level low that that has a \nclosed circulation instead of a faster moving and open trough. \nIn the latter scenario, the warm and moist southerly winds \ngenerally stay in eastern Kansas and points east; and this is \nwhy the winters are climatologically so dry here. The median\nprecipitation for the 15-October to 28-February time frame (4.5\nmonths) is only 3.1\" at Dodge City. Additionally, another upper\nlevel trough is forecast to amplify across the eastern USA, \nforcing north winds and drier air into the tropics. So despite a\nreturn to south winds in western Kansas, north winds will \npersist at lower latitudes so that the return flow won't be as \nmoist as one might expect. There may be enough instability for \nscattered showers and t-storms as the upper trough and \nassociated cold front move east by Friday or Saturday; but \nwidespread heavier rains are unlikely. This is especially true \ngiven the absence of a low level cold dome across the plains in \nadvance of the upper trough that might promote moist ascent. \nThat said, the arrival of this trough is still at least a week \naway; and there is plenty of time for a switch to a more robust \nsolution. The grand ensemble shows even lower chances (10%) for \n.5\" or greater of rain across southwest Kansas than it showed \nyesterday (15-25%).",
